## 1. Introduction
Singlet oxygen $(^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , the excited state of oxygen, has been extensively studied due to its unique chemical properties and wide applications in both selective oxidation reactions and photodynamic therapy.[1-4] One path for ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ production is the oxidation of superoxide radical $(\mathrm{O}_{2}\cdot\bar{\cdot})$ by photogenerated hole. However, the generation of other reaction oxide species, such as hydroxyl radical (·OH), $\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{\cdot}$ -, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , unavoidably accompany this process, resulting in a decrease in selectivity for some organic oxidation reactions [5, 6] Alternatively, ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ can also be produced by energy transfer from excited photosensitizers. Specifically, the photosensitizer is excited to singlet state, which is then further converted to the triplet state through intersystem crossing (ISC) process. After that, energy transfer occurs from the triplet state to the ground state oxygen $(^{3}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , giving rise to ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ .[7-9] Normally, the formation efficiency of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ is closely related to the efficiency of the ISC process.[7] The effective strategies for improving the ISC process mainly focus on the following two aspects: first, introducing heavy atoms to enhance the spin-orbit coupling effect;[10,11] and second, reducing the energy gap between singlet state and the triplet state to improve the yield of triplet excited state.[12]
As we all know, porphyrins are an important kind of photosensitizer that exhibit several superior features, such as broad absorption from UV to visible light, structural flexibility, easy chemical modification. [13-16] However, the direct application of molecular porphyrin displays disadvantages, namely, low efficiency and difficulty in recycling, etc. One effective approach is to transfer the homogenous porphyrin into heterogeneous catalyst. From this perspective, porphyrinbased metal organic frameworks (MOFs) come into view. On the one hand, the strong coordination between metal atom and porphyrin may improve the chemical stability and simplify the recycling process as MOFs is insoluble in most neutral solvents. [17-18] On the other hand, the spin-orbit coupling effect may be strengthened in the presence of some metal dots, which consequently promote the ISC process.[10,19-20]
Ag-based semiconductors display excellent photocatalytic performance. [21,22] However, the easily reduction to metallic Ag by photogenerated electrons is a big block towards practical application. How to stabilize the $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ based photocatalysts is still a challenge as far as we know. In our previous report, it is proved that the exciton effect can avoid the generation of photogenerated electrons, which effectively suppress the reduction of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ and improve the stability. [23] Nevertheless, to the best of our knowledge, few reports investigate the exciton effect between porphyrin and $\mathrm{Ag}$ and the corresponding influence on the stability of $\mathrm{Ag}$ .
Based on above considerations, we choose 5,10,15,20-tetra(4-pyridyl) porphyrin (TPyP) as the target molecule.[24] N atoms on the pyridine ring can strongly coordinate with ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ ions, and the structure of AgTPyP two-dimensional (2D). As expected, the photocatalytic results suggest that AgTPyP shows nearly $100\%$ conversion and selectivity towards the selective oxidation of sulfides to sulfoxides. Furthermore, AgTPyP also shows a significant effect on killing HeLa cells in vitro under visible light irradiation. Importantly, AgTPyP displays excellent stability during the photocatalytic process. Both the electron spin resonance (ESR) and the in-situ reactive oxygen species capture results suggest that ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ is the only active oxygen species. The higher $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ absorption capacity and a narrow energy gap between $\mathrm{T}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{S}_{1}$ for AgTPyP result in a higher production of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ than that for TPyP. This work provides an alternative approach to simultaneously enhance the exciton effect of porphyrin and stabilize $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ based photocatalysts.
## 2 Materials and methods
## 2.1 Synthesis of ${_{A g T P y P}}$
$0.05\:\mathrm{g}\:\mathrm{TPyP}$ was dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ chloroform $\mathrm{(CHCl_{3})}$ ), denoted as solution A. $0.339\mathrm{g}\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ was dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, denoted as solution B. Solution B was slowly dropped to solution A. A purplish-brown precipitant was formed, which was filtered and washed with deionized water and ethanol for several times, then dried in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.2 Characterization
X-ray power diffraction (XRD) data of the as-prepared samples were collected using Bruker AXS D8. Their morphologies were analyzed by scanning electron microscope (SEM) measurements (Hitachi, S4800). The UV/Vis diffuse reflectance spectra (UV/Vis DRS) were measured using a Shimadzu UV 2550 recording spectrophotometer equipped with an integrating sphere and $\mathrm{BaSO_{4}}$ as a reference. The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were carried out using a Thermo Fisher Scientific (ESCALAB, 250XI) X-ray photoelectron spectrometer and the results were calibrated using the adventitious C 1s peak at $284.8~\mathrm{eV}$ . Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were carried out by a Bruker ALPHA-T spectrometer. Nitrogen-adsorption isotherms were collected at $77\mathrm{K}$ by a high-performance specific surface and microspore analyzer (Builder, KuboX1000). The surface area was calculated by Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method. The $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption curves were measured on the gas adsorption analyser (Builder, PCA-1100) in an ice bath. The samples were pre-treated under vacuum at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.3 Photooxidation of methyl phenyl sulphides
$7\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst was added to a $15~\mathrm{mL}$ quartz reactor containing $2{\mathrm{~mL}}$ acetonitrile. $10\:\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ methyl phenyl sulfide was dropped into the suspension, which was stirred for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ to reach the absorption-desorption equilibrium. To ensure this reactor with sufficient $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , pure oxygen was bubbled into the reactor for $15~\mathrm{min}$ . The reactor was exposed to visible light $\lambda{>}420~\mathrm{nm}$ , PLSSXE300, Beijing Perfectlight Technology Co., LTD, China) for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at $15~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The products were characterized by a gas chromatography (Shimadzu GC2030).
## 2.4 Capture of singlet oxygen
$0.675\mathrm{mg}p$ -nitrosodimethylaniline (RNO) and $49.02\mathrm{mg}$ imidazole (ID) were dissolved in $90\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water.[25] $5\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst was added in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ above solution under stirring, which was then exposed to visible light $\lambda>420\mathrm{nm}$ , 300W Xe arc lamp). $2\mathrm{mL}$ suspension was taken every 30 min. The supernatant was obtained by centrifugation, and absorption spectra was carried out to determine the absorption intensity at $440\ \mathrm{nm}$ (the maximum absorption of the oxide product by singlet oxygen) with a spectrophotometer (Shimadzu UV 2550).
## 2.5 Electron spin resonance measurements
2 mg catalyst and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ TEMP (2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine, the singlet oxygen capture agent) were dispersed in $2{\mathrm{~mL}}$ acetonitrile. After being illuminated under UV/Vis light for a certain time, the suspension was tested using an ESR spectrometer (JEOL JES-X320) operating on the X-band frequency $(9.4~\mathrm{GHz})$ at room temperature. $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ·- and ·OH were detected using $0.01\mathrm{~M~}$ DMPO (dimethyl pyridine N-oxide) as capture agent, except that ethanol and water were used as the solvent, respectively.
## 2.6 Cell Culture
Human cervical cancer HeLa cell line was purchased from Shanghai Zhong Qiao Xin Zhou Biotechnology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). HeLa cells were cultured in a $25\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ tissue culture flask with RPMI-1640 medium (GE Healthcare Life Sciences, Utah, US) including $10\%\mathrm{(v/v)}$ fetal bovine serum (certified FBS, Biological Industries, Israel) and $2\%$ penicillin/streptomycin ( $\mathrm{10000\U/mL}$ and $100\mathrm{mg/mL}$ , respectively, Solarbio, China) in a $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ atmosphere and $95\%$ air at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ 500 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Trypsin-EDTA $(0.25\%)$ phenol red (Gibco, Canada) was used to split HeLa cells and then seeded the cells in new flasks or treatment wells.
## $2.7I n$ vitro cytotoxicity assay
The in vitro cytotoxicity of the prepared samples was investigated on the human HeLa cells. At first, HeLa cells were incubated in the $25\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ cell-culture flask with 6 mL DMEM media. Then 200 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of cell solution (20000 cells/well) was seeded into a 96-well plate by detaching from the flask. Following incubation, the cells further incubated upon addition of different concentration of $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ , from $0.01~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ to $100~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , and then incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ After further incubation overnight, the WST-1 cytotoxicity assay kit was added to the 96-well plate. The cell viability was calculated on the ratio of the absorbance of the wells at $492\mathrm{nm}$ , which was measured by POTENOV PT-3502B.
## 2.8 Calculation methods
The present first principle calculations were performed by Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP)[26-27] with the projector augmented wave (PAW) method.[28] The generalized gradient approximation (GGA) of Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) functional combined with Grimme’s dispersion correction[29] is used to process the exchange-functional. Optimize calculations of atoms and cell optimization are performed on the basis of setting the cut-off energy of the plane-wave basis at $500~\mathrm{eV}$ . The size for the optimized model used for the theoretical calculation is $19.76{\times}7.66{\times}38.33\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ , which is very close to the experimental results ( $19.86\times8.07\times38.22\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}.$ ). In particular, lattice constants a and c are very close to the experimental values, while that of b changes the most, which decreases to $94.91\%$ of the experimental value. In addition, volume is also compared between the experimental and optimized models, and the optimized value is about $94.71\%$ of the experimental one. These deviations are within reasonable errors. The $1\times2\times1$ Gamma $\mathbf{k}$ -point sampling for the structures was applied in the Brillouin zone integration. A convergence energy threshold of $10^{-5}\mathrm{eV}$ was used for the self-consistent calculations. The equilibrium lattice constants are optimized under the condition that the maximum force on each atom is within $0.05\mathrm{eV}/\mathrm{\AA}$ .
In order to obtain the exciton binding energy, the 122-atom model contains one full porphyrin (TPyP)-4Ag-4Pyridine subunit was applied (Fig. S1). The ground state structure was optimized by Gaussian 09[30] at PBE0-D3BJ/def2-SV(P) level [29,31-32], followed by TD-PBE0 calculation at the ground state geometry. The Multiwfn program [33] was employed for calculation of exciton binding energies. The SOCMEs were obtained by ORCA 4.2.0 program,[34] without the TDA approximation.
## 3 Results and discussion
The XRD patterns of the as-prepared AgTPyP are shown in Fig. 1a. The diffraction peaks of AgTPyP are consistent with that in previous reports,[35] which belongs to the orthorhombic phase with space group Pbcm. The structure of AgTPyP demonstrates a 2D topology layers that are stacked via a wave-like form (Fig. S2-3). The morphology of AgTPyP is observed by SEM images as showed in Fig. 1b. The as-prepared AgTPyP possesses a cuboid morphology. The element mapping spectra demonstrate the uniform distribution of C, N and Ag. To obtain more structural information, FT-IR spectra (Fig. 1c) for AgTPyP and TPyP are further investigated. The peak at $970\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the C-N stretching vibration in the porphyrin ring,[36] and it does not shift for AgTPyP compared with that for TPyP, which indicates that $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ ions are not inserted into the porphyrin ring. The characteristic absorption peak of the pyridine ring moves from $1590~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for
TPyP to $1597~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for AgTPyP,[36] which is caused by the increase of conjugation due to the formation of a 2D structure. The surface chemical composition and element chemical valence state are analyzed by XPS. A survey spectrum for $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ , shown in Fig. S4, confirms the presence of Ag, C, N and O atoms. As shown in Fig. 1d, the signal of $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ 1s for TPyP contains three peaks at $398.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ , 399.2 and $401.4\mathrm{{eV}}$ , corresponding to the $-\mathrm{{N=},\mathrm{{N_{pyridyl}}}}$ and -NH-, respectively.[37] For AgTPyP, compared with the results that the peaks of $-N=$ and -NH- remain the same as that for TPyP, the binding energy for the $\mathrm{{N_{pyridyl}}}$ shifts towards higher binding energy $(399.6{\mathrm{~eV}})$ , further suggesting the coordination between $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ atoms. The BET surface area is characterized by nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms (Fig. 1e). The framework structure significantly increases the specific surface area from $9.73~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for TPyP to $41.08\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for AgTPyP.
The UV/Vis DRS are measured to determine the optical absorption properties of $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ and TPyP as shown in Fig. 1f. Porphyrins generally exhibit two strong absorption bands: the Soret (or
B) band ( $380{\cdot}450\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) and Q Band $500{-}750~\mathrm{nm}$ . The Q band is typically considered to be the transition to the lowest excited singlet state $\mathrm{(S_{1})}$ , and the B band is the transition to the second excited singlet state $(\mathsf{S}_{2})$ .[38] Compared with the peaks (512, 554, 586 and $645~\mathrm{nm}$ ) of Q band for TPyP, AgTPyP still retain four peaks, further indicating that $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ ion does not coordinate with $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ in the porphyrin ring. In addition, the peaks display bathochromic shift to 530, 569, 600 and $657\mathrm{nm}$ , suggesting that the energy gap between ground state $\mathrm{(S_{0})}$ and $\mathrm{S}_{1}$ is narrowed due to the coordination with Ag. Similarly, the Soret band of AgTPyP is also red-shifted and is split into two peaks (368 and $446\mathrm{nm}$ ), which is mainly due to the exciton coupling between the porphyrin units.[39]
To check the performance of $\mathbf{Ag}\mathrm{TPyP}$ and TPyP, the photocatalytic-oxidation of phenyl methyl sulfide is examined under visible $\lambda{>}420\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) light irradiation (Scheme 1). In the presence of AgTPyP, the conversion and selectivity are close to $100\%$ after 1 hour, which are significantly higher than the corresponding conversion $(13\%)$ and selectivity $(25\%)$ over TPyP under the same conditions (Fig. 2a). In addition, compared with vacuum or air, $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ atmosphere can greatly improve the conversion efficiency (Fig. S5), suggesting that $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is a prerequisite for this reaction. As shown in Table 1, AgTPyP still maintains a high conversion and selectivity for the phenyl methyl sulfide with different substituents (-Br, -Cl and - $\mathrm{\cdotCH}_{3}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) at the para position, indicating that AgTPyP is universal for the selective photooxidation of sulfides to sulfoxides.
