To explore the cause of the photocatalytic activity, it is necessary to check the reactive oxygen species (ROS). Obviously, the conversion dropped to $0\%$ after the addition of TEMP, while the addition of the other three capture agents displays negligible effect on the conversion. These results clearly point out that ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ is the only ROS responsible for the selective oxidation from sulfides to sulfuroxide. Moreover, ESR trapping measurements are carried out to identify the photogenerated ROS directly. Under illumination, the characteristic signal of 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine-N-oxyl (TEMPO) with an intensity of 1:1:1 is obtained due to the oxidation of TEMP by ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ (Fig. 2c-d). The intensity of TEMPO is strengthened with the extension of irradiation time, indicating that ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ is gradually produced under illumination. Compared with TPyP, the signal increase over AgTPyP is faster, indicating that AgTPyP is more efficient with respect to singlet oxygen production. To verify that only ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ is produced over AgTPyP, the ESR spectra of $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotOH}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}\cdot\mathbf{\bar{\mu}}$ are also checked. As expected, no obvious signal is detected (Fig. S6-7). The RNO-ID method is further implemented to confirm the higher capability of AgTPyP than TPyP with respect to ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ production. The oxidation of RNO by singlet oxygen would decrease the absorption at $380\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Fig. S8, a faster decrease can be observed in the presence of AgTPyP than that in the presence of TPyP, verifying the faster ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ production over the former.
As an excellent photosensitizer for singlet oxygen, AgTPyP is further checked in terms of in vitro photodynamic therapy. HeLa cells are used as the target cells, which are incubated in the presence of either TPyP, AgTPyP or $\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with the concentration varying from $0.01{-}100~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ (Fig. S9). Compared to the other two photocatalysts, AgTPyP displays a little effect on cell viability in the dark. After 24 h’s incubation, the system is exposed to visible light. As shown in Fig. S10, an obvious cell death (cell viability $=35\%$ ) can be observed after $10\mathrm{min}$ irradiation at the concentration of $100~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ . However, TPyP and $\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ exhibit nearly no photodynamic therapy effect under the same conditions. These results indicate that $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ , which can efficiently generate ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , holds a large potential in the application of photodynamic therapy.
To check the stability of AgTPyP, five cycles of oxidation test of phenyl methyl sulfide are performed. As can be seen in Fig. 3a, there is no significant reduction in terms of both the conversion efficiency and selectivity for each cycle. In addition, the XRD (Fig. 3b), FT-IR (Fig. 3c) and XPS spectra (Fig. 3d) after five cycles display no distinguishable difference from the fresh sample. It is noteworthy to point out that nearly no $\mathrm{Ag^{0}}$ is generated according to the analysis of XPS Ag 3d spectrum, and the reduction of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ ion to metallic Ag is a common phenomenon for Ag based semiconductors during the photocatalytic process as mentioned above.[22] These results show that
AgTPyP demonstrates excellent stability.
To find the reason for the higher activity of AgTPyP, the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption curves are collected at $273\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. 4a). The maximum adsorption capacity of $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ is $31.2\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ under 1 atm, which is 4.4 times that of TPyP $(7.1\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ ). These results show that AgTPyP provides more active sites for $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ adsorption, which facilitates the transfer energy from excited $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ to oxygen. The higher $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption maybe a possible reason for the higher performance of AgTPyP.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations are further used to explore the density of states (DOS) and band structure of AgTPyP (Fig. 4b and Fig. S11). The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) of AgTPyP is composed of Ag 4d, N 2p, O 2p and C 2p orbitals, while the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) is composed of C 2p and $\textsf{N}2\mathsf{p}$ orbitals. The absent contribution of $\mathrm{Ag}$ orbital to the LUMO, is believed to be the essential reason for the excellent stability of AgTPyP during the photocatalytic processes. The energy level diagrams of TPyP and AgTPyP are further calculated to reveal the effect between $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ ions and TPyP. As shown in Fig. 4c-d, both the energy of the lowest triplet state $\left(\mathrm{T}_{1}\right)$ for TPyP and $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ are larger than the energy required to ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation $(92\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , about $0.96\mathrm{eV}$ ), indicating that both TPyP and AgTPyP are able to produce ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ . However, compared with TPyP, it is easier for the ISC process to occur for $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ , due to the smaller energy gap of between $\mathbf{S}_{1}$ and $\mathrm{T_{1}}\left(\Delta\mathrm{E_{Sl-T1}},\Delta\mathrm{E_{Sl-T1}}{=}0.88\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{E_{S1-T1}=}0.93\ \mathrm{eV}$ for TPyP). In addition, the spin-orbit coupling constant $(\xi)$ [40] between $\mathbf{S}_{1}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{1}$ for AgTPyP $(0.28\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ is larger than that of TPyP $(0.20\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ ), which further suggests that the structure of AgTPyP is more advantageous for the ISC process. These features cause the increase of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ production. Moreover, the exciton binding energy is further calculated, which is slightly larger for AgTPyP $(4.08\mathrm{eV})$ than that for TPyP $(4.05\mathrm{eV})$ . The result of exciton binding energy also justifies the higher ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ generation over AgTPyP.
## 4 Conclusions
In conclusion, we designed and synthesized AgTPyP with a 2D structure. Compared with ligand TPyP, AgTPyP displays a more efficient ability to generate ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ . High conversion and selectivity are obtained over AgTPyP towards the photocatalytic oxidation of sulfides to sulfoxides. AgTPyP also displays higher efficiency towards the deactivation of HeLa cell for the in vitro photodynamic therapy. The better performance of $\mathbf{AgTPyP}$ is ascribed to the presence of $\operatorname{Ag}$ , which not only improves the adsorption site of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , but also enhances the ISC process via increasing the exciton binding energy and reducing the energy gap between singlet and triplet state. In addition, unlike the other Ag based semiconductors, AgTPyP displays high stability during the photocatalytic process.
